# Detailed magnetic study on the formato-bridged MOFs with anion-tunable magnetic properties

1State Key Laboratory of Coordination Chemistry; Nanjing National Laboratory of Microstructures; School of Chemistry and Chemical Engineering, Nanjing University, Nanjing 210093, China 2Beijing National Laboratory for Molecular Sciences; State Key Laboratory of Rare Earth Materials Chemistry and Applications; College of Chemistry and Molecular Engineering, Peking University, Beijing 100871, China
Detailed studies of the structures, magnetic properties and photodimerization of a series of formato-bridged MOFs with the general formula $\mathrm{M}_{2}(\mathrm{HCOO)}_{3}(4,4^{\prime}{\cdot}\mathrm{bpe})_{3}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{3}(\mathrm{X})$ $(4,4^{\prime}{\cdot}\mathrm{bpe}=4,4^{\prime}$ -bipyridylethylene, ${\bf M}={\bf M}{\bf n}\left({\bf1}{-}{\bf X}^{-}\right)$ , $\mathrm{X^{-}=C l O_{4}^{-}}$ , $\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{BF}_{4}^{-}$ , $\Gamma^{-}$ , $\mathrm{Br}^{-}$ ; $\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{C}\mathbf{o}$ $(2{-}X^{-})$ , $\mathrm{X}^{-}=\mathrm{ClO}_{4}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}$ ; $\mathbf{M}=Z\mathbf{n}$ (3-X), $\mathrm{X}^{-}=\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-},$ ) were reported. Careful magnetic measurements on an oriented single crystal of $\mathbf{1-ClO}_{4}^{-}$ determined the spin-flop magnetic phase diagram and some intrinsic parameters, such as the intralayer coupling $J.$ , the anisotropy field $H_{\mathrm{A}}$ and the exchange field $H_{\mathrm{E}}$ . Different anions can remarkably tune the magnetic properties of ${\bf1-X}^{-}$ , especially the critical fields of the spin-flop transition. Compound $2\mathrm{-ClO}_{4}^{-}$ remained paramagnetic down to $2\mathrm{K}$ .
metal formate, spin-flop, magnetic phase diagram, photodimerization
## 1  Introduction
Molecule-based magnetic materials have attracted intense interest in recent years due to the fundamental researches and the development of new functional materials [1–5]. Parallel to the achievements such as the high $T_{\mathrm{c}}$ magnets [6–8], large coercivity and high magnetization [9, 10], single-molecule and single-chain magnets [11–14] and so on, there is increasing interest in the multifunctional materials, which combine magnetism and other properties, such as conductivity [15], optical properties [16], porosity [17] and ferroelectric properties [18]. These materials present the possible synergism of different functions and thus new potential applications.
One of the main emphases on the design strategy is to search for good bridging ligands that can effectively mediate the magnetic coupling between the local spin carriers. As we have already highlighted in our former article [19], the three-atom ligands, such as the azido $(\mathbf{N}_{3}^{-})$ [19–21], the formate (HCOO ) [19, 22] and so on, were proved to be good candidates to construct molecular magnets. They are particularly interesting in several aspects. First, they are not too big for magnetic coupling and can always transfer moderate or strong magnetic interaction, ferromagnetic or antiferromagnetic. Second, they show rich and vivid coordination characteristics and bridging modes to transition metal centers, which makes them capable to construct various interesting structures with efficient magnetic interactions. This will dramatically benefit the discovery of new molecular magnets. Third, these three-atom bridges have an important feature, more or less in common, that they will usually preclude the inversion center between the two bridged metal ions. It will symmetrically allow the asymmetric interaction; and weak-ferromagnetism (WF) due to spincanting can thus be reasonably anticipated for many three-atom bridged compounds [19, 23–26].
In the past few years, we have synthesized a series of interesting molecular magnetic materials by using three-atom bridges, ranging from zero-dimensional single-molecule magnets to the three-dimensional magnets [19, 22, 27–32]. For the formato-bridged compounds, three-dimensional [AmineH] $\mathbf{M}(\mathrm{HCOO})_{3}]$ [30–33] and $[\mathbf{M}_{3}(\mathbf{HCOO})_{6}](\mathbf{guest})_{n}$ [22, 34] (AmineH+ is ammonium cations such as $\mathrm{NH}_{4}^{+}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}^{+}$ , $\mathrm{(CH_{3})_{2}N H_{2}^{+}}$ , $\mathrm{CH_{3}C H_{2}N H_{3}^{+}}$ and $(\mathrm{CH}_{2})_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{2}^{+}$ and so on; $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}=\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{n}}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ , ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ , ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ , $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ) present two very interesting series. Among them, $[\mathbf{M}_{3}(\mathrm{HCOO})_{6}](\mathrm{guest})_{n}$ is a unique family of the magnetic porous materials with flexible frameworks. In addition to the magnetic ordering, these flexible frameworks show absorption of a wide spectrum of both hydrophilic and hydrophobic guests through singlecrystal-to-single-crystal transformation; and these guests can subtly adjust the $T_{\mathrm{C}}$ and even the chirality of the frameworks. Recent results indicate that the [AmineH][ $\mathsf{M}(\mathrm{HCOO})_{3}]$ 3D systems are also very good platforms for the construction of a series of multiferroics [18, 31, 32, 35]. Furthermore, by introducing a trivalent iron ion into the system, three $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}$ based complexes $\mathrm{\small{[NH_{2}(C H_{3})_{2}][F e^{I I I}M^{I I}(H C O O)_{6}]}}$ $(\mathbf{M}=\mathrm{Fe}$ Mn, and Co) could be synthesized and show some interesting magnetic properties [36]. Besides these simple frameworks without any coligands, another series of compounds $\mathrm{M(HCOO)}_{2}(4,4^{\prime}{\cdot}\mathrm{bpy)(H_{2}O)}_{n}$ $\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}$ , Co, $n=0$ ; $\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{C}\mathbf{o}$ , Ni, $n=5$ ) were also synthesized and investigated [37]. The bridging coligand was proved to be very important to the formation of some particular architectures and to the structure modification. As a continuing research along this line, we introduced another bridging coligand $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bpe ( $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipyridylethylene) into the formato-bridged systems. As a rigid bridge, $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bpe has been widely employed in the construction of self assembled and porous materials and also in the molecular magnetism [38–40]. Compared with $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bpy, $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bpe has its own advantages: it is longer than $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bpy and might lead to bigger porosity; and furthermore it can bring some additional functionalities into the system since the olefin group $\scriptstyle(\mathrm{CH=CH})$ ) provides other properties, such as the photo or heat induced dimerization, photo induced isomerization and so on [41–43].
In light of these aspects discussed above, we synthesized a series of formato-bridged MOFs: $\mathrm{M}_{2}(\mathrm{HCOO})_{3}(4,4^{\prime}-$ bpe) ${\phantom{}_{3}(\mathrm{H}_{2}O)}_{3}(\mathrm{X})$ $\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}$ (1-X), $\mathrm{X}^{-}=\mathrm{ClO}_{4}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{BF_{4}^{-}}$ , $\Gamma^{-}$ , $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Br}}^{-}$ ; $\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{C}\mathbf{o}$ (2-X), $\mathrm{X^{-}=C l O_{4}^{-}}$ , $\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}$ ; $\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}$ (3-X), $X^{-}=$ $\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-})$ . Compounds $\mathbf{1-X^{-}}$ show interesting magnetic properties and photoreactivity and their magnetic properties can be remarkably tuned by the $X^{-}$ anions. We have briefly reported the magnetic property and photoreactivity of $\mathbf{1-ClO_{4}^{-}}$ in a communication [44]. Here, we report the detailed studies on the magnetic properties of $\mathbf{1-ClO_{4}^{-}}$ based on the measurements on an oriented single crystal and the influences of the anions on their magnetic properties of $\mathbf{1-X^{-}}$ .
## 2  Experiments
## 2.1 General remarks
## 2.1.1  Synthesis
A 6 mL of $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ containing $0.3\ \mathrm{mmol}$ of $\mathbf{MX}_{2}$ (or $0.3\ \mathrm{mmol}$ of $\mathbf{MCl}_{2}$ and $0.6\ \mathrm{mmol}$ of NaX/KX in case we didn’t find the $\mathbf{MX}_{2}$ in our lab; the $\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$ anion didn’t influence the syntheses of these materials) and $0.6\mathrm{mmol}$ of $\mathrm{NH_{4}H C O O\cdot H_{2}O}$ was added to $5~\mathrm{mL}$ of hot DMF containing $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bpe (0.30 mmol). The solution was heated to around $50–60\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , stirred for about 5 minutes and filtrated. Slow evaporation of the solution gave the column-like crystals of the desired compounds, yield about $50\%-60\%$ . The elemental analysis results and the main IR absorption bands are listed in Table 1.
## 2.1.2  Solid-state photoreaction
A crystalline sample of $\mathbf{1-ClO}_{4}^{-}$ or $\pmb{3-N O_{3}^{-}}$ $({\sim}30\ \mathrm{mg})$ was placed in a water-cooled beaker and was irradiated using an $\mathrm{Hg}$ lamp $(400~\mathrm{W})$ for approximately 2 days. For $\pmb{3-N O}_{3}^{-}$ , the irradiated sample can be directly dissolved in hot D6-DMSO for the $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR. But for $\mathbf{1-ClO_{4}^{-}}$ , the $\mathbf{M}\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ need to be removed first. The irradiated sample of $\mathbf{1-ClO}_{4}^{-}$ was dissolved in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ of methanol, and then $20~\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathrm{\DeltaNaOH}$ was added into the solution. After being stirred for 30 minutes, the dark brown precipitation was removed by filtration. The methanol was removed under reduced pressure and the residue was washed with a small amount of water. After drying in the air, the product (a mixture of $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bpe and $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -tpcb, tpcb $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ tetrakis (4-pyridyl)-cyclobutane) was dissolved in D6-DMSO and checked with $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR ${200}~\mathrm{MHz}$ , 298 K). Both spectrums are displayed in Figure S1 and S2 with the following peaks: 8.58 (d, Py–H of $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bpe), 8.33 (d, Py–H of $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -tpcb); 7.60 (d, Py–H of $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bpe), 7.55 (d, $\scriptstyle\mathrm{CH=CH}$ of $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bpe), 7.20 (d, Py–H of $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -tpcb), 4.64(s, CH–CH of $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -tpcb). The yield was estimated to be less than $60\%$ from the integral intensity of the peaks in NMR.
## 2.2 Structure determinations
All these compounds are isostructures with small differences in the unit cell parameters. So, we collected the full datasets for only $\mathbf{1-ClO}_{4}^{-}$ , $\mathbf{1-NO}_{3}^{-}$ , $\boldsymbol{{\mathbf{1}}}\boldsymbol{\mathbf{-}}\boldsymbol{\mathbf{\check{I}}}^{-}$ and $2\mathrm{-ClO}_{4}^{-}$ . For others, we only checked their unit cell parameters. All data collections were made on a Nonius Kappa-CCD with Mo $\mathsf{K a}$ radiation $(\lambda=0.71073\mathrm{~\AA~})$ at $293\mathrm{~K~}$ . The structures were solved by direct methods and refined by a full matrix least squares technique based on $F^{2}$ using the SHELXL 97 program [45]. The hydrogen atoms of $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bpe and $\mathrm{\:HCOO^{-}}$ were added to the calculated positions. The hydrogen atoms of water were all added from the differential map or fixed to calculated positions. More details of the data collections are presented in Table 2. The crystal structures of these four complexes have been deposited at the Cambridge Crystallographic Data Center and allocated the deposition numbers
## 3  Results and discussion
## 3.1 Crystal structures
All eight compounds are isomorphism (monoclinic, $P2_{1}/\mathrm{c})$ and have a 3D pillared layer framework with $\mathrm{M}{\cdot}\mathrm{HCOO}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ herringbone layers along the $a b$ plane connected by $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bpe pillars along the $c$ axis. The $X^{-}$ anions and lattice $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bpe molecules reside in the cavities of the framework (Figure 1). For the completeness of this work, we describe again the structure here, using $\mathbf{1-ClO_{4}^{-}}$ as an example. The details of the rest structures can be found in the CIF files. As shown in Figure 1(a), there are two unique octahedral $\mathbf{M}\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions (Mn1 and $\mathbf{M}{\mathbf{\mathrm{n}}}2$ ), both of which are equatorially coordinated by four O atoms from three different formates and one water, and are axially coordinated by two $\mathbf{N}$ atoms from two $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bpe. Connected by formates of two different bridging modes (syn-anti for O1-C1-O2 and O5-C3-O6, and anti-anti for O3-C2-O4), the $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{n}}^{2+}$ ions form the 2D layer. One lattice water molecule (O9) lies in the window of the 2D network, and forms H-bonds with the metal-coordinated water and O1 of the formate bridges, resulting in dense layers. Due to the long pillar $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bpe, the interlayer Mn–Mn distances are quite long (ca. $14{\bar{\mathrm{\AA}}}{\cdot}$ , which results in a 2D magnetic system. The lattice water O9 and the staggering of adjacent layers blocks all possible channels of the framework and leads to the formation of two distinct pseudo-cavities in sites A and B (Figure $1(\boldsymbol{\mathrm{b}},\boldsymbol{\mathrm{c}})$ and S3).
Site A, surrounded by three $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bpe pillars, contains the $\mathrm{ClO}_{4}^{-}$ anion, which forms weak $\mathrm{O^{\cdot\cdot\cdotH-C}}$ bonds with its peripheral $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bpe. Although different $X^{-}$ anions can be easily included in the framework of $\mathbf{1-X}^{-}$ by choosing different starting materials in the synthesis, they are not exchangeable in-situ. This is reasonable for the non-accessible A sites. These anions of different sizes can subtly influence their structures, and thus their magnetic properties as shown below. Site B, occupied by a lattice $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bpe, is surrounded by four $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bpe pillars in a manner such that two pairs of $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bpe are nearly orthogonal to each other. The lattice $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bpe is face-to-face to one pair of the $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bpe of the box but edge-to-face to another. The former leads to parallel stacking $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bpe columns along the $b$ axis (Figure 1(d)). The olefins of adjacent $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bpe $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{C}$ groups) are roughly parallel with the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C-C}}$ distances of $3.883-4.143\mathrm{~\normalfont~\AA~}$ for $\mathbf{1-ClO_{4}^{-}}$ . This structural feature satisfies the geometric criterions of Schmidt for $[2+2]$ photodimerization, namely, parallel alignment and olefin separation of $<4.2\mathring\mathrm{\mathrm{A}}$ [41, 46].
UV irradiation of the crystalline samples of $\mathbf{1-ClO}_{4}^{-}$ and $\pmb{3-N O}_{3}^{-}$ for 2 days indicates that they are photoactive involving $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bpe to give tpcb ( $\mathrm{^{1}H\ N M R}$ in d6-DMSO: 4.64 ppm for the $\mathrm{CH-CH}$ protons in $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -tpcb). Even though the XRD patterns of $\mathbf{1-ClO_{4}^{-}}$ remained almost unchanged after the irradiation (Figure S4), we did not accomplish the reaction from single crystal to single crystal because the reaction results in the cracking of the crystals. Since the completeness of the reaction is only about $60\%$ and some other changes happened during the reaction (the samples turns dark yellow for $\mathbf{1-ClO}_{4}^{-\cdot}$ , the magnetic properties of the irradiated samples are far from understandable.
## 3.2 Magnetic properties
To get a clear view of the magnetic properties of this series of compounds $\mathbf{1-X^{-}}$ , we performed detailed magnetic measurements on the powders of $\mathbf{1-ClO_{4}^{-}}$ , $\mathbf{1-NO}_{3}^{-}$ , $\mathbf{1-BF}_{4}^{-}$ , $\boldsymbol{{\mathbf{1}}\cdot\boldsymbol{\mathbf{I}}}^{-}$ and on one oriented single crystal of $\mathbf{1-ClO_{4}^{-}}$ . An antiferromagnetic state with a field induced spin-flop transition was proved to be the ground state of $\mathbf{1-ClO}_{4}^{-}$ and its magnetic phase diagram was established by series of careful measurements. Furthermore, the anions with different sizes were proved to have some subtle influences on their magnetic
properties.
## 3.2.1  Magnetic study upon $\pmb{I}\pmb{-C}l O_{4}^{-}$
With the guide of the rotation results, we measured the magnetic properties (temperature dependent susceptibility $\chi(T)$ and field dependent magnetization $M(H)$ at $2\mathrm{~K~}$ ) with the external field $H//c$ and $H\bot c$ and also on the powder (Figure 3). All $\chi(T)$ curves above $20~\mathrm{K}$ superpose together but a significant anisotropy emerges below $5\mathrm{~K~}$ . $\chi_{11}$ shows a pronounced peak at $3.5\mathrm{~K~}$ and decreases toward zero on further cooling; $\chi_{\perp}$ remains almost constant after reaching the maximum at $3.5\mathrm{~K~}$ . Meanwhile, $\chi_{\mathrm{powder}}$ shows a peak at $3.5~\mathrm{K}$ and reaches $0.63~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ at $2\mathrm{~K~}$ , close to the value $0.59\mathrm{\cm^{3}m o l^{-1}}$ of $(2\chi_{\perp}+\chi_{//})/3$ . This behavior is consistent with the above result that $\mathbf{1-ClO}_{4}^{-}$ is an antiferromagnet with $c$ as the easy-axis. The Néel temperature $T_{\mathrm{N}}$ deduced from the peak position of $\mathrm{d}\chi_{//}T/\mathrm{d}T$ is $2.5\mathrm{~K~}$ . Fitting of $\chi^{-1}$ above $50\mathrm{K}$ with the Curie-Weiss law gives $C=8.65~\mathrm{cm^{3}~m o l^{-1}~K}$ and a negative $\theta=-3.5\ \mathrm{K}$ indicating an intralayer $A F$ coupling. Well documented examples show that the magnetic coupling mediated by anti-anti and syn-anti formates can be antiferromagnetic [19, 22, 30–37]. If we consider these two formates to transmit similar coupling, the susceptibility for the Heisenberg antiferromagnet $(S=5/2)$ of a (6, 3) network $(z=3$ and $p_{4}=2$ , see Ref. 46) can be fitted by Rushbrook and Wood’s HTS model using the eq. (1) with the Hamiltonian $\begin{array}{r}{H=-2J\Sigma_{\langle i,j\rangle}S_{i}\cdot S_{j}}\end{array}$ [47]:
$$
\chi=\frac{N g^{2}\beta^{2}S(S+1)}{3k_{\scriptscriptstyle B}T}\biggl(1+\sum_{n=1}^{6}C_{n}x^{n}\biggr)^{-1}
$$
Where $x=J/k_{\mathrm{{B}}}T$ , and $C_{1}=-17.5$ , $C_{2}=110.8333$ , $C_{3}=$ 304.1111, $C_{4}~=~-991.8287$ , $C_{5}=9346.140$ , and $C_{6}=$ $364381.4.N,J,g,\beta,S,k_{\mathrm{B}}$ and $T$ have their usual meanings. The best fit of $\chi_{\mathrm{powder}}$ above $20~\mathrm{K}$ gives the intralayer coupling $J=-0.21~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $g=2.02$ with $R=3.1{\times}10^{-5}$ $[R=$ $[\Sigma(\chi_{\mathrm{obs}}-\chi_{\mathrm{calc}})^{2}/\Sigma(\chi_{\mathrm{obs}})^{2}]$ , consistent with the results reported for Mn-O-C(R)-O-Mn interactions $_{(-0.2}$ to $-0.3~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}.$ ) [19, 22, 30, 37].
For the $M(H)$ measurements at $2\mathrm{~K~}$ (Figure 3, inset (b)), the $M_{\perp}$ curve was almost a straight line reaching $7.55~\mu_{\mathrm{B}}$ at $50\mathrm{kOe}$ , lower than the saturated $10~\mu_{\mathrm{B}}$ for $\left[\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{2}\right]$ . In the low field region $(H<10~\mathrm{kOe}$ ), $M_{//}$ increases more slowly than $M_{\perp}$ does; and after an abrupt increase, it goes coincident with $M_{\perp}$ until the field reaches about $40\ \mathrm{kOe}$ , where a second change, though less pronounced, happens. These two transitions can be confirmed by the two sharp peaks in the in-phase signal of the field dependence of the ac susceptibility $\chi(H)$ up to $70\ \mathrm{kOe}$ on the powder, which are consistent with the peaks of $\mathrm{d}M/{\mathrm{d}H}$ (Figure S5). This $M(H)$ behavior is typical for a spin-flop transition, which often occurs in antiferromagnets with small anisotropy when the external field is parallel to the easy-axis [48]. From Figure S5, the critical fields of the transition for $\mathbf{1-ClO_{4}^{-}}$ at $1.8\mathrm{~K~}$ are determined to be $H_{\mathrm{SF}}=12.8\mathrm{kOe}$ and $H_{\mathrm{c}}{=}45.8\mathrm{kOe}$ .
To further investigate the spin-flop transition of $\mathbf{1-ClO_{4}^{-}}$ we measured the detailed $M_{//}(H)$ with the field up to $70\mathrm{kOe}$ at different temperatures $\left.T=1.9\ –2.9\ \mathrm{K}\right.$ , step $0.1\mathrm{~K~}$ , Figure 4(a)) and $\chi_{//}(T)$ under different fields $\boldsymbol{\mathcal{H}}=1$ , 5, 10, 12, 15 kOe, Figure 4(b)). Below $T_{\mathrm{N}},$ , all $M_{//}$ curves have two transitions, which become less pronounced when the temperature increases and disappear above $T_{\mathrm{N}}$ . From the peaks of the dM/dH, series values of the critical fields $(H_{\mathrm{SF}},T)$ and $(H_{\mathrm{C}},$ T) can be obtained. These data determine part of the phase boundaries in the $_{H-T}$ plane. The $\chi_{//}$ curves under different fields also show some regular changes. When $H<H_{\mathrm{SF}}$ , all $\chi_{11}$ curves show a peak and decrease monotonously towards zero. The curves shift to the low temperature along with the increasing fields. When $H$ is larger than $H_{\mathrm{SF}}$ $(H=15{\mathrm{~kOe}})$ , the $\chi_{11}$ increases again at low temperatures after reaching a round peak at about $3\mathrm{~K~}$ . This behavior is just like the $\chi_{\perp}$ at $1\ \mathrm{kOe}$ , in consistent with the fact that the spins under 15 kOe flop to the direction perpendicular to the field. The derivatives of $\chi_{//}$ curves give some other critical points, $(H,$ T), in the $_{H-T}$ plane and complete the whole phase diagram of $\mathbf{1-ClO_{4}^{-}}$ (Figure 5). The filled squares and open circles are from the $M_{//}(H)$ and $\chi_{I/}(T)$ curves respectively, and the dotted lines are added subjectively. Like a typical phase diagram for a spin-flop transition, there are three different regions: AF, SF, and P, respectively. The region AF corresponds to the antiferromagnetic phase with the spins parallel to the $c$ axis. With fields applied along $c$ , increase of the temperature or field will lead to the phase transition to the spin-flop phase (SF region), and eventually to the paramagnetic phase (P region).
With the phase diagram, we can estimate two intrinsic parameters $H_{\mathrm{E}}$ (internal exchange field) and $H_{\mathrm{A}}$ (the anisotropy field) [48]. Upon application of an external field $H_{//}$ on an antiferromagnet, a competition is set up between $H_{//}$ and $H_{\mathrm{E}}$ . For an antiferromagnet with weak anisotropy, when $H_{//}$ reaches a critical field $H_{\mathrm{SF}}$ , the anti-parallel magnetizations on the two sublattices flop from the direction of the easy-axis to that perpendicular to it. A further increase of $H_{//}$ to $H_{\mathrm{C}}$ will totally align the spins to the direction of the field, which makes the system to the saturated paramagnetic state. In the molecular field model, the $H_{\mathrm{E}}$ and $H_{\mathrm{C}}$ are related to the critical fields at $T=0~\mathrm{{K}}$ , $H_{\mathrm{SF}}(0)$ and $H_{\mathrm{{C}}}(0)$ , by the following equations [48]:
$$
\begin{array}{r}{\bar{H_{\mathrm{sF}}(0)}=(2H_{\mathrm{E}}H_{\mathrm{A}}+H_{\mathrm{A}}^{2})^{1/2}}\\ {H_{\mathrm{c}}(0)=2H_{\mathrm{E}}-H_{\mathrm{A}}}\end{array}
$$
From the extrapolation of the phase diagram down to $0\mathrm{K}$ , we can estimate the critical fields at $0\mathrm{K}$ : $H_{\mathrm{SF}}(0)\approx14~\mathrm{kOe}$ , $H_{\mathrm{C}}(0)\approx45~\mathrm{kOe}$ . Thus, the $H_{\mathrm{E}}$ and $H_{\mathrm{A}}$ could be estimated to be about $24.5~\mathrm{kOe}$ and $3.9\mathrm{~kOe}$ . The ratio $\alpha=H_{\mathrm{A}}/H_{\mathrm{E}}$ , a relative measure of the ideality of the isotropic exchange interaction, is estimated to be 0.16. With the equation $\boldsymbol{H_{\mathrm{E}}}=$ $z|J|S/g\mu_{\mathrm{B}}$ , we can also get the intralayer exchange coupling $J{=}\ensuremath{-}0.30\ensuremath{\mathrm{cm}^{-1}}$ , which is close to the value obtained before.
The spin-flop transition has been widely observed and investigated in the classic magnetism and molecular magnetism. Since both the $H_{\mathrm{C}}$ and $T_{\mathrm{N}}$ are proportional to the magnetic interaction, it is not very easy to observe $H_{\mathrm{C}}$ under normal lab conditions. If the coupling is too strong, a too big $H_{\mathrm{C}}$ is probably needed to overwhelm the $A F$ interactions to align all the spins parallel in a saturated paramagnetic state. This situation happens in our formal reported compound $\mathbf{Mn}(\mathbf{N}_{3})_{2}(\mathbf{bt})_{2}$ where $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{n}}^{2+}$ ions are bridged by the $E E$ azido [49]. However, if the interaction is too weak, the $T_{\mathrm{N}}$ for the ordered state will be too low. For compound $\mathbf{1-ClO_{4}^{-}}$ , the formate ligand can transmit moderate magnetic interactions. Due to this suitable coupling, the $T_{\mathrm{N}}$ and $H_{\mathrm{C}}$ are both lower than $\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{N}_{3})_{2}(\mathrm{bt})_{2}$ , falling in the measurement range of our SQUID system.
## 3.2.2  Magnetic investigations on the powders of $\pmb{I}\pmb{-}\pmb{X}^{-}$
All magnetic data on powders for $\mathbf{1-ClO_{4}^{-}}$ , $\mathbf{1-NO}_{3}^{-}$ , $\mathbf{1-BF}_{4}^{-}$ and $\boldsymbol{{\mathbf{1}}{-}}\boldsymbol{{\mathbf{I}}}^{-}$ , including the $\chi(T)$ at $1\ \mathrm{kOe}$ (Figure S6), the fieldcooled (FC) susceptibility at $200\mathrm{Oe}$ (inset of Figure 6(a)), the ac susceptibility $\chi(T)$ $\mathrm{\Delta}H_{\mathrm{dc}}=0$ Oe, $H_{\mathrm{ac}}=2$ Oe, Figure 6a), the $M(H)$ at $2\mathrm{~K~}$ (Figure S7), and the field dependence of $a c$ susceptibility $\chi(H)$ at $1.8\mathrm{~K~}$ (Figure 6(b)) were measured. Some important magnetic parameters are displayed in Table 3. Although the Curie constants are the same to be $8.65~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{mol}^{-1}~\mathrm{K}.$ , the Weiss constants vary from $-3.5$ to $-5.3\mathrm{~K~}$ . Using the same HTS model and equation 1, the $\chi(T)$ above $50~\mathrm{K}$ can be fitted well (Figure S6) and the $J$ values are from $-0.21$ to $-0.28~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . All the FC and $\chi^{\prime}(T)$ curves have a peak in the range of 3.5 to $4\mathrm{~K~}$ , indicating a phase transition. No detectable out-of-phase signals or frequency dependence were observed. From the peaks of the $\mathrm{d}\chi T/\mathrm{d}T$ , the $T_{\mathrm{N}}$ can be deduced, varying from 2.5 to $3.0\mathrm{K}$ . The $M(H)$
curves at $2\mathrm{~K~}$ for $\mathbf{1-X^{-}}$ increase almost linearly, with the maximum magnetization $M_{\mathrm{max}}$ at $50\mathrm{kOe}$ decreasing gradually from $\mathbf{1-ClO_{4}^{-}}$ to $\pmb{1-\mathrm{I}}^{-}$ . The spin-flop transitions are still obvious and they are further proved by the peaks in the $\chi^{\prime}(H)$ curves, which correspond to the critical fields $H_{\mathrm{SF}}$ and $H_{\mathrm{C}}$ (Figure 6(b)). There are no obvious differences in $H_{\mathrm{SF}}$ but the $H_{\mathrm{C}}$ increases obviously from $\mathbf{1-ClO}_{4}^{-}$ to $\mathsf{\mathbf{1}}\mathsf{-\mathbf{I}}^{-}$ .
From these experiments, we can draw two conclusions. First, different anions do not change the basic magnetic properties of the framework since all the curves and parameters are very similar to each other. This fact is somewhat obvious since these anions do not participate in the magnetic coupling directly. The second and more interesting point is that the anions do have some obvious influences on their magnetic properties. As the size of the anions diminishes from $\mathbf{1-ClO_{4}^{-}}$ to $\mathbf{1-NO}_{3}^{-}$ and $\mathbf{1-BF}_{4}^{-}$ and to $\boldsymbol{{\mathbf{1}}{-}}\boldsymbol{{\mathbf{I}}}^{-}$ , the $\lvert\boldsymbol{\theta}\rvert,T_{\mathrm{N}},J,$ and especially $H_{\mathrm{C}}$ , increase gradually, the $M_{\mathrm{max}}$ decreases gradually, and the $H_{\mathrm{SF}}$ stays almost unchanged (Table 3). The sizes of the anions have the opposite influence on the $\vert\theta\vert,T_{\mathrm{N}},H_{\mathrm{C}}$ and $|J|$ : the bigger the anion is, the smaller these parameters are. These changes might originate from the structure modifications of different anions. Careful analysis of the structure reveals that the Mn-Mn distances change slightly according to the anions. The interlayer Mn-Mn distances between Mn1-Mn2, Mn2-Mn1A, Mn1A-Mn2A decrease with the decrease of the size of the anions (6.19, 6.37, $5.16\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ for $\mathbf{1-ClO}_{4}^{-}$ ; 6.17, 6.32, 5.14 Å for $\mathbf{1-NO}_{3}^{-}$ ; 6.17, 6.36, $5.13\mathrm{~\AA~}$ for 1-I). These small decreases may increase the interlayer coupling $J$ slightly, particularly at very low temperature near $0\mathrm{~K~}$ . Since $H_{\mathrm{C}}$ is proportional to $H_{\mathrm{E}}$ and thus to $J$ , increase of $J$ will increase remarkably the critical field $H_{\mathrm{C}}$ from $45.8\mathrm{kOe}$ for $\mathbf{1-ClO}_{4}^{-}$ to $56.8\mathrm{kOe}$ for $\boldsymbol{{\mathbf{1}}\cdot\boldsymbol{\mathbf{I}}}^{-}$ .
## 3.2.3 Magnetic property of $2\cdot C l O_{4}^{-}$
Although it is isostructural to $\mathbf{1-X^{-}}$ , compound $2\mathrm{-ClO}_{4}^{-}$ only shows the paramagnetic behavior down to $2\mathrm{K}$ . Displayed in Figure 7 are the $\chi T(T),\chi^{-1}(T)$ at $1\ \mathrm{kOe}$ and $M(H)$ at $2\mathrm{~K~}$ for $\left[\mathbf{Co}_{2}\right]$ unit. Upon cooling from $300\mathrm{K}$ , the $\chi T$ value decreases monotonously from $6.4~{\mathrm{cm}}^{3}~{\mathrm{mol}}^{-1}~{\mathrm{K}}$ till $2\mathrm{~K~}$ . The CurieWeiss fit of the high temperature data gives $C=7.07~\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ $\mathrm{mol^{-1}~K}$ and $\theta=-30.5\mathrm{~K~}$ . This big negative $\theta$ definitely should have some contributions from the spin-orbit coupling for the $^{4}T_{1\mathrm{g}}$ state for a ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ center and also indicate the AF coupling between the ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ centers. The $M(H)$ increases almost linearly with no obvious abnormality, reaching 4.1 $\upmu_{\mathrm{B}}$ at $70\ \mathrm{kOe}$ . Furthermore, the field dependent magnetization at a $d c$ field of $200\mathrm{Oe}$ (Figure S8) and the ac susceptibility under a zero $d c$ field (Figure S9) were also measured. No sharp peaks were found in these figures, clearly excluding the antiferromagnetic phase transition for $2\mathrm{-ClO}_{4}^{-}$ .
## 4 Conclusions
As our ongoing investigations on the three-atom bridged molecular magnets, we presented here a series of multifunctional metal-organic frameworks with $\mathbf{M}^{2+}$ ions bridged by $\mathrm{\Pi{HCOO}^{-}}$ . By introducing the potential functional coligand $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bpe, these MOFs can have two distinct properties: magnetism and photoreactivity. Solid-state $[2+2]$ photodimerization and antiferromagnetism with well-defined spin-flop transition were both observed in compounds $\mathrm{\underline{{Mn_{2}(H C O O)_{3}(4,4^{\prime}-b p e)_{3}(H_{2}O)_{3}(X)}}}$ $\mathrm{(X=ClO_{4}^{-}}$ , $\mathrm{NO}_{3}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{BF}_{4}^{-}$ , $\Gamma^{-}$ , $\mathrm{Br}^{-}$ ). The magnetic properties of $\mathbf{1-ClO}_{4}^{-}$ were carefully investigated by detailed measurements on a well oriented single crystal. The spins in the antiferromagnetic state were confirmed to be parallel to the normal of the Mn-HCOO layer. The phase diagram of the spin-flop transition was established; and the exchange field $H_{\mathrm{E}}$ , the anisotropy field $H_{\mathrm{A}}$ , the intralayer coupling $J$ , and the anisotropy parameter $\alpha$ are estimated to be $H_{\mathrm{E}}=24.5~\mathrm{kOe}$ , $H_{\mathrm{A}}=3.9~\mathrm{kOe}$ , $J=$ $-0.21~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , and $\alpha=0.16$ , respectively. Furthermore, the framework has one pseudo-cavity being selectively filled by anions with variable size, which remarkably can tune their magnetic properties, especially the spin-flop critical fields.
